Chapter: ListGrowth

  cites Foreword chapter Console

 COMPILER-42 arm 2, the growth harness, and this file is its CALIBRATION.

 A pass/fail arm cannot tell linear from quadratic, so the harness records
 heap allocation at N, 2N and 4N and reports the RATIO between successive
 sizes (L-THRESHOLD: count the signature rather than threshold the
 magnitude). Linear roughly doubles per doubling; quadratic roughly
 quadruples.

 Two arms with known answers, because a harness that has only ever been
 pointed at the subject cannot be shown to work. `build-lin` is an ordinary
 accumulator loop. `build-quad` copies the whole accumulator before every
 push, so it allocates the triangular number of cells by construction. If
 the ratios these two produce are not clearly apart, the instrument cannot
 answer the question the design is asking and no reading from it means
 anything.

 Allocation is measured with `__heap-save`, which returns the bump pointer.
 Bare metal has no GC and nothing is reclaimed inside a call, so the
 difference between two reads IS the allocation total over the span.

Section: Body

  build-lin : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  build-lin (n) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else build-lin (n - 1) (list-push acc n)

  copy-into : List Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  copy-into (src) (i) (dst) =
    if i >= list-length src then dst
    else copy-into src (i + 1) (list-push dst (list-at src i))

  build-quad : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  build-quad (n) (acc) =
    if n <= 0 then acc
    else build-quad (n - 1) (list-push (copy-into acc 0 []) n)

  grow-lin : Integer -> Integer
  grow-lin (n) =
    let h0 = __heap-save
    in let xs = build-lin n []
    in let h1 = __heap-save
    in (h1 - h0) + (0 * list-length xs)

  grow-quad : Integer -> Integer
  grow-quad (n) =
    let h0 = __heap-save
    in let xs = build-quad n []
    in let h1 = __heap-save
    in (h1 - h0) + (0 * list-length xs)

  classify : Integer, Integer -> Text
  classify (small) (big) =
    let pct = if small <= 0 then 0 else (big * 100) / small
    in if pct < 300 then "LINEAR" else "QUADRATIC"

 The verdict is the RATIO between successive sizes, never the byte counts.
 A raw count is a fact about today's allocator and goes red the first time
 anything about allocation changes, which trains a reader to ignore the
 arm; the ratio is the property under test and holds across that. The cut
 at 300 sits between the two measured populations (about 199 and about 396)
 and is nowhere near either.

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("lin  100->200 : " & classify (grow-lin 100) (grow-lin 200))
    print-line-uni ("lin  200->400 : " & classify (grow-lin 200) (grow-lin 400))
    print-line-uni ("quad 100->200 : " & classify (grow-quad 100) (grow-quad 200))
    print-line-uni ("quad 200->400 : " & classify (grow-quad 200) (grow-quad 400))
  end
